  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Turning more warm and humid through Thursday, \nwith increasing thunderstorm chances, before high pressure \nreturns for the weekend.\n\nTranquil conditions continue today under the influence of high\npressure, which will traverse the region today before moving\noffshore Wednesday. As this occurs, clockwise flow around the \ndeparting high will usher in a southerly wind on Wednesday. \nSome afternoon gusts up to 20 mph are possible depending on the \ndegree of vertical mixing. High temperatures begin to approach \nthe 90 degree mark, which comes with a gradual uptick in dew \npoints/humidity levels. This is accompanied by the expected \nincrease in overnight warmth.\n\nAn upper trough currently crossing southern Saskatchewan is\nlikely to impact the local weather by Wednesday and Thursday.\nWhile much of Wednesday should be dry, some weak/modest\nperturbations in the flow ahead of the trough could bring some \nevening/overnight showers. \n\nThe more notable forcing from the aforementioned trough does \nnot arrive until Thursday. A mixture of thermodynamic \ningredients are in place for this event, but it is difficult to \nsay if these will align or not. While instability levels \nincrease with the uptick in low-level moisture, vertical shear \ngenerally averages around 20 to 25 knots. Some isolated severe \nthunderstorms are possible, but the overall setup lends itself \nto a number of question marks. However, Thursday does look like \na typical late August day with a front encroaching on the \nregion. This would be characterized by numerous showers and some\nthunderstorms, many of which will bring heavy downpours and \nfrequent lightning. Any thunderstorm threat may linger into the \nnight with the cold front being a late arriver and in no\nparticular hurry to clear the region.\n\nHeading into Friday and the weekend, an upper-level trough \nbegins to move directly over our forecast area. With highs \nremaining in the mid 70s to 80s, warm and humid conditions \nreturn to our region. This airmass brings with it chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms Friday afternoon and evening,\nespecially along/east of the slowly departing cold front that\nwill likely still be somewhere in the forecast area. The front\nis expected to clear by Friday night into the weekend. \n\nOn Saturday and Sunday, the upper air wave pattern flattens into \nzonal flow. Depending on whether this synoptic pattern holds, \nSaturday and Sunday could see a few diurnally driven showers \nand thunderstorms.",
